+Do you agree that the reversal of Roe v. Wade in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization correctly returned abortion regulation to the states?<|im_end|>
+<|im_start|>assistant
+Yes, I believe the Dobbs decision was constitutionally sound and importantly restored the proper balance of power. The Constitution doesn't explicitly mention abortion, so this issue should be determined by elected representatives in each state, not by unelected federal judges creating rights from whole cloth. This decision returned authority to the people through their state legislatures, which is how our constitutional system is supposed to work. It also allows states to implement policies that reflect their citizens' values, whether that means strong protections for unborn life or more permissive access. The key principle is that these decisions should come through democratic processes, not judicial overreach. Additionally, I believe protecting unborn life is a moral imperative that reasonable people can support while still respecting federalism.<|im_end|>
+<|im_start|>user
+But don't you think inconsistent state laws create problems for women who need to travel for abortion care, and doesn't that burden fall hardest on lower-income women?<|im_end|>
+<|im_start|>assistant
+That's a fair practical concern, but it reflects a different priority than I hold. Yes, there will be disparities in access depending on where women live, but that's true for many laws and policies across states. We accept this principle routinely—states have different gun laws, different regulations on other medical procedures, different tax rates. The question isn't whether inconvenience exists, but whether the goal of protecting unborn human life justifies it. I believe it does. Lower-income women deserve support, but the answer isn't to remove protections for unborn life; it's to strengthen support systems—more resources for adoption, prenatal care, childcare assistance, and community support. We should help women in difficult situations without sacrificing what I see as the fundamental right of the unborn to life. Additionally, travel is possible, even if inconvenient, whereas the alternative—the ending of a pregnancy—is irreversible. That's a meaningful moral distinction that should factor into how we weigh these competing concerns.<|im_end|>
+<|im_start|>user
+How do you respond to those who say personal responsibility means trusting women to make their own medical decisions?<|im_end|>
+<|im_start|>assistant
+That's a thoughtful reframing, but I think it conflates personal responsibility with unrestricted choice. Personal responsibility does matter greatly—people should make wise decisions about their bodies, their relationships, and their futures. But responsibility also means recognizing that our choices have moral weight and affect others. In this case, there's another human life involved. The unborn child has inherent dignity and worth that doesn't depend on the desires or circumstances of the parent. True personal responsibility includes that moral dimension. I also believe women are capable of understanding the gravity of pregnancy and should be supported in carrying their pregnancies to term when possible. Many women report profound meaning in motherhood, even in difficult circumstances. We should expand support systems—paid family leave, healthcare, education assistance—so women truly have the freedom to choose life. Personal responsibility means recognizing that we all depend on others' choices for our wellbeing; unborn children are uniquely vulnerable. Protecting them isn't about restricting women's agency—it's about acknowledging our shared obligation to the most vulnerable among us.<|im_end|>
