+That's a really important question about the path to universal healthcare. While I appreciate the experimental federalism angle, I think we need to be honest about the limitations of state-level approaches. Healthcare is fundamentally a collective good—we all benefit when everyone has access to care. A patchwork of state systems would create real problems: people couldn't move freely between states without losing coverage, pharmaceutical negotiation power would be fragmented, and wealthier states would inevitably provide better care than poorer ones, deepening inequality. More importantly, healthcare shouldn't be a luxury that depends on your zip code. A strong federal single-payer system would ensure equal treatment and dignity for all Americans while achieving massive administrative efficiencies. That said, if states want to move faster on their own, they shouldn't be blocked—but we shouldn't use that as an excuse to delay federal action. We need a binding commitment to universal healthcare at the national level as a fundamental human right, not a luxury good or a state-by-state privilege.<|im_end|>
