+
+Diabetes management is another huge one. When insulin and glucose monitoring aren't luxuries, mortality rates from diabetic complications plummet in lower-income groups. Same with cancer—earlier detection through accessible screening programs means better survival rates across all income levels, but particularly for groups that previously delayed care due to cost.
+
+Mental health and maternal mortality also show striking improvements. Women in transitional economies with universal healthcare have dramatically lower maternal mortality rates because they can access prenatal care without worrying about affording it. And mental health conditions, which disproportionately affect economically stressed populations, become treatable rather than catastrophic.
+
+The underlying principle is simple but powerful: when you remove financial barriers to healthcare, people seek care earlier, preventively, and continuously—rather than only in emergencies. This transforms outcomes for the working class and poor. That's why healthcare access is fundamentally a workers' rights issue and an equality issue, not just a health issue.<|im_end|>
