+
+Look at countries like Germany, France, and Canada: their citizens pay less in total healthcare costs—both through taxes and direct payments—than Americans do. A middle-class family in the U.S. might pay $15,000 annually in premiums, deductibles, and copays combined. That money would be reconfigured into progressive taxation where people pay according to their ability.
+
+As for inefficiency, here's what's actually inefficient: our current system spends 15-25% on administrative overhead, billing bureaucracy, and profit margins. A unified public system eliminates redundant billing departments, reduces paperwork nightmares, and focuses resources on actual care. When healthcare isn't treated as a profit center, efficiency naturally improves—providers focus on health outcomes, not maximizing revenue extraction.
+
+Workers would also benefit tremendously. Employers wouldn't bear healthcare costs, which would strengthen their ability to invest in wages and job creation. The psychological burden of medical debt anxiety would lift for millions of families.
+
+This isn't theoretical—it works in wealthy democracies across the world. The question is whether we value collective wellbeing over corporate profit margins.<|im_end|>
