+
+Here's the reality: developing nations aren't inherently dependent on accepting waste and maintaining low environmental standards. That's a structural inequality we've created. With proper support, they can leapfrog to cleaner technologies.
+
+The solution is progressive funding. Wealthy nations and corporations that have profited from this inequality should finance the transition—both as reparative justice and practical investment. That means:
+
+- Debt relief for countries transitioning away from waste-processing industries
+- Technology transfer and training programs funded by the Global North
+- Direct financial support for green manufacturing infrastructure
+- Fair trade agreements that don't penalize sustainable practices
+- Support for workers transitioning from hazardous industries into better-paying, safer jobs
+
+We can't solve this with market forces alone—we need government intervention and international cooperation. Countries like Costa Rica have shown that strong environmental standards can coexist with economic development when coupled with investment and policy support.
+
+The real cost is paid by workers and communities breathing toxic air and drinking contaminated water. That's unconscionable. Justice requires bearing the true cost upfront.<|im_end|>
