+
+However, you're right that wealthy nations can't unilaterally solve this. The answer is genuine partnership based on equity:
+
+- Support capacity-building so developing nations can enforce their own environmental standards without economic punishment
+- Create regional markets for recycled materials, giving nations economic incentive to process waste sustainably
+- Enable countries to develop their own environmental policies without pressure to prioritize foreign corporations' interests
+- Hold ALL actors accountable—governments, corporations, and workers—through transparent, democratically-controlled mechanisms
+
+Environmental justice isn't about the Global North managing the Global South. It's about ending exploitation and creating conditions where all communities can thrive sustainably. That requires redistribution of resources and power, yes—because that's what justice demands.
+
+Developing nations with adequate support have repeatedly shown they prioritize environmental protection. Trust and investment work far better than condescension.<|im_end|>
